## 2. Methodological Rigor (4.5/10) — Major Concerns

### Critical issues

- **No inferential statistics anywhere.** All results are descriptive (medians, percentages)
  with no confidence intervals, p-values, effect sizes, or statistical tests. With stochastic
  decoding (temperature=0.7), observed differences may be within noise range.

- **Single run per cell.** Each of the 240 observations is a single stochastic generation.
  Without repeated runs, findings are unreproducible.

- **No inter-annotator agreement** reported for the 3-annotator human evaluation. This is a
  critical omission — non-negotiable for papers reporting human judgments.

- **Response-level metric-human correlations are weak** (FK: -0.40, SMOG: -0.15) but omitted
  from the paper. Only configuration-level rank agreement (n=4, statistically meaningless) is
  reported, giving an overly optimistic picture of metric reliability.

- **Length confound uncontrolled.** Shorter responses mechanically score lower on readability
  formulas. The paper does not disentangle genuine simplification from brevity artifacts.

### Moderate issues

- All four readability metrics are highly correlated formula-based indices from the 1940s-70s;
  no modern NLP-based complexity metrics used.
- A1 vocabulary coverage rate is never measured despite being the direct target of the
  weighting intervention.
- Weight factor selected on the same 15-prompt set used for the main experiment (risk of
  overfitting; should use a separate validation set).
- The 15-prompt count in the paper conflicts with 16 prompts in experiment_configs.py.

