## 4. Related Work Coverage (3/10) — Critical Gap

This is the paper's most urgent problem. The related work cites only 5 NLP papers and
completely omits literature on readability-controlled text generation and the broader
readability/simplification field that this workshop covers.

Note on task framing: this paper does Readability-Controlled Text Generation (RCTG) —
generating simple text from a prompt — not classical Automatic Text Simplification (ATS),
which rewrites existing complex text. The citations below are tiered accordingly. See
Section 7 for per-paper task analysis.

Note: Nie et al. (2023), already cited in Section 2.1, IS an RCTG paper (keywords → sentence
with hard CEFR-level vocab constraints, training-time). Its task differs from this paper
(keywords-to-sentence vs. question-to-answer, hard vs. soft constraints), but it is not ATS.
The assessment that the paper "cites zero RCTG papers" is therefore wrong — Nie et al. is
one. However, it is narrow (structured keyword input) and training-time, so the gaps
identified below remain valid.

### Tier 1 — Critical: same task as this paper (RCTG), missing entirely

- **Malik et al. (2024) — From Tarzan to Tolkien: Controlling Language Proficiency Level**
  ACL Findings 2024. https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-acl.926/
  Generates new educational content at target CEFR levels (A1–C2) for language learners —
  the same task formulation as this paper. Finds that sub-7B models struggle at A1. This
  paper directly addresses that gap with inference-time interventions. Not citing it is the
  most significant novelty-positioning gap in the current draft.

- **ReadCtrl (Tran et al., 2024) — Readability-Controlled Instruction Learning**
  arXiv:2406.09205. https://aclanthology.org/2025.in2writing-1.3/
  Fine-tunes Mistral-7B to generate text at continuous readability levels via instruction
  tuning. Same task as this paper, training-time approach. Provides the direct
  training-time vs. inference-time contrast the paper needs to sharpen its contribution.

### Tier 2 — Mandatory for venue credibility: ATS papers named in the workshop CFP

These are ATS papers (rewriting existing text, not generating from scratch). They must
be cited because reviewers at this workshop will expect them, but should be introduced
with explicit acknowledgment of the task distinction.

- **Kew et al. (2023) — BLESS: Benchmarking Large Language Models on Sentence Simplification**
  EMNLP 2023. https://aclanthology.org/2023.emnlp-main.821/
  Benchmarks 44 LLMs on sentence rewriting. Establishes that instruction-tuned LLMs respond
  to simplification prompts without fine-tuning — the methodological premise this paper
  builds on. *Explicitly named in the workshop CFP.*

- **Farajidizaji et al. (2024) — Is It Possible to Modify Text to a Target Readability Level?**
  LREC-COLING 2024. https://aclanthology.org/2024.lrec-main.815/
  Zero-shot GPT-3.5/GPT-4 prompted to rewrite existing texts to numerical readability targets.
  Methodological surface similarity is high (prompting for readability targets) but the task
  is ATS. Their finding that "readability is bounded by source text complexity" contrasts
  favorably with this paper's generation approach, which has no such bound.
  *Explicitly named in the workshop CFP.*

- **Cripwell et al. (2023) — Context-Aware Document Simplification**
  ACL Findings 2023. https://aclanthology.org/2023.findings-acl.834/
  Document-level ATS using discourse context. Weakest fit for this specific paper — the
  "context" concept is different (discourse coherence vs. generation-guiding prompts). Cite
  in one sentence alongside Kew et al. as ATS landscape background; do not frame as
  closely related. *Explicitly named in the workshop CFP.*

### Tier 3 — Important: ATS papers with findings directly applicable to this paper's results

- **Barayan et al. (2025) — Analysing Zero-Shot Readability-Controlled Sentence Simplification**
  COLING 2025. https://aclanthology.org/2025.coling-main.452/
  ATS task, but two findings directly corroborate this paper: (1) LLMs struggle to rewrite
  to A1 level, which contrasts with this paper showing A1 generation is more achievable;
  (2) "formula-based metrics misrepresent perceived simplicity" — directly supports this
  paper's human evaluation finding. Best cited in the human evaluation discussion section.

- **Agrawal & Carpuat (2023) — Controlling Pre-trained LMs for Grade-Specific Simplification**
  EMNLP 2023. https://aclanthology.org/2023.emnlp-main.790/
  Fine-tuned model for grade-level ATS. Belongs alongside MCTune in the existing
  training-time methods subsection as another grade-specific training-time approach.

### Missing model citations (mandatory for reproducibility)

- **TinyLlama** — Zhang et al. (2024). arXiv:2401.02385
- **Phi-3-mini** — Abdin et al. (2024). arXiv:2404.14219
- **Qwen2.5** — Qwen Team (2024). arXiv:2412.15115
- **Qwen3** — Qwen Team (2025). arXiv:2505.09388

### Missing contextual references

- TSAR 2025 Shared Task Findings — Readability-Controlled Text Simplification.
  https://aclanthology.org/2025.tsar-1.8/
  Community benchmark context; prompting-based approaches outperform fine-tuned systems,
  consistent with this paper's findings.

- arXiv:2511.05080 (Nov 2025) — Architectural heterogeneity in LLM readability control.
  Directly parallels the paper's architectural heterogeneity discussion section.

### Fundamental problem

The paper cites one RCTG paper (Nie et al., 2023 — keywords-to-sentence generation with CEFR
vocabulary constraints) but misses the two most relevant RCTG comparisons: Malik et al. (same
open-ended generation task, language learners, CEFR levels, but large models) and ReadCtrl
(same generation task, training-time approach). Without these, the paper cannot anchor its
novelty claim against the closest prior work. Additionally, the three ATS papers named in the
CFP (Kew, Farajidizaji, Cripwell) are absent, signalling unfamiliarity with the venue's
community regardless of the task distinction.

