## 5. Novelty & Significance

**Novelty: 6/10** (revised from 4/10 once the task distinction is properly articulated)

What is genuinely novel:
- The 2×2 factorial separating prompting from decoding manipulation for RCTG (not ATS) is
  not replicated elsewhere.
- Malik et al. (2024) — the closest prior work on the same task — uses only prompting and
  fine-tuning with large models, and finds sub-7B models fail at A1. This paper's logit
  weighting intervention is novel in the generation setting and directly addresses that gap.
- Demonstrating A1-level generation with 0.6B parameters (Qwen3) is a concrete,
  reproducible result in a regime prior work found infeasible.
- ReadCtrl achieves generation-level readability control only via fine-tuning (Mistral-7B);
  this paper is the first to do so at inference time on sub-4B models.

What is not novel (still applies):
- Vocabulary-based logits boosting is a documented HuggingFace feature; similar soft-penalty
  approaches have been applied to ATS (MDPI Symmetry 2025).
- Prompting dominance over decoding manipulation is consistent with existing CTG literature
  and TSAR 2025 shared task results.
- Architectural heterogeneity in controllability was studied in arXiv:2511.05080 (Nov 2025)
  for larger models — differentiation via the sub-4B focus is still needed.

**Significance: 5/10**

Strengths:
- SLMs for educational text generation is genuinely underserved — most work uses large models.
- Sub-1B and ~1B models for resource-constrained deployments is a relevant practical niche.
- 2x2 factorial design is methodologically clean for a workshop paper.

Weaknesses:
- No SARI metric — but SARI measures quality of rewriting operations (add/keep/delete)
  against reference simplifications, so it does not apply to this generation task. The
  weakness is the absence of any generation-appropriate cross-study metric (e.g., BERTScore
  against reference A1 answers, or A1 vocabulary coverage rate).
- No external generation baselines — only compares against no-intervention control. A
  zero-shot GPT-4 condition or a ReadCtrl-style fine-tuned model would provide meaningful
  cross-system comparison.
- "Educational application" framing is thin: no actual deployment, student interaction, or
  pedagogical evaluation.

